    "evidence_text": "Memory and Modularity in Cell-Fate Decision Making\n\nGenetically identical cells sharing an environment can display markedly different phenotypes.\n\nThe monoallelic expression appeared random and dynamic because there was considerable variation among closely related embryonic cells.\n\nSimilar patterns of monoallelic expression were observed in mature cells.\n\nWe conclude that independent and stochastic allelic transcription generates abundant random monoallelic expression in the mammalian cell.\n\nFor genes that should not be expressed in a particular cell type, protein output is lowered to inconsequential levels.\n\nNature, Nurture, or Chance: Stochastic Gene Expression and Its Consequences\n\nSingle-RNA counting reveals alternative modes of gene expression in yeast\n\nHow precisely the transcription machinery fulfills this task is not known.\n\nWe found that expression levels for particular genes are higher than initially reported and can vary substantially among cells.\n\nHowever, variability for most constitutively expressed genes is unexpectedly small.\n\nThese data directly demonstrate the existence of multiple expression modes used to modulate the transcriptome.\n\nEpigenetic instability in ES cells and cloned mice.\n\nThese data imply that even apparently normal cloned animals may have subtle abnormalities in gene expression.\n\nThe role of regulatory variation in complex traits and disease\n\nCommon recommendations for cell line authentication, annotation and quality control fall short addressing genetic heterogeneity.",
